<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Heracles</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Herakles, Hercules</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Heracles, called Hercules by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, was the greatest hero of Greek myth, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> and the mortal Alcmene. He was famed for his strength and for the Twelve Labours imposed on him by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eurystheus">Eurystheus</a>, and after his death he was received among the gods. Many Greek cities and royal houses, including the Spartan kings and the Argead kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a>, claimed descent from him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/heracles.jpg" alt="Heracles" width="320" height="560" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Glycon of Athens (copy), Lysippos (original type) · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Herakles_Farnese_MAN_Napoli_Inv6001_n01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The kings worshipped the Greek pantheon shown on their coins, Zeus, <b>Herakles</b>, Athena and Apollo, but also took part in Indian religions: Heliodorus, ambassador of Antialcidas, called himself a devotee of Vāsudeva on his pillar at Vidisha around 113 BCE, the first known inscription of the Bhagavata movement,…</blockquote><a href="/empire/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek Kingdom · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Greek tradition, preserved by Herodotus, held that a dynasty descended from <b>Heracles</b> ruled Lydia for twenty-two generations, about 505 years, which would place its beginnings around 1200 BCE, but this early history is legendary and archaeology at Sardis shows only gradual growth after the collapse of Hittite power.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-lydia">Kingdom of Lydia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Herodotus and Thucydides record the legend that the Argead kings of Macedon descended from Temenus, king of Argos, and so from <b>Heracles</b> and Zeus.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-macedon">Kingdom of Macedon · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Kushan coins show a remarkable mix of gods, from the Iranian Mithra and the goddess Nana to Shiva, <b>Heracles</b> and the Buddha, labelled in Bactrian as Boddo.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kushan-empire">Kushan Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Greek tradition explained the arrival of Dorian Greeks as the return of the Heraclidae, the descendants of <b>Heracles</b>, and the two Spartan royal houses, the Agiads and the Eurypontids, claimed descent from him.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sparta">Sparta · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Beyond the Olympians, the Greek world was full of divine powers: nymphs of springs and trees, river gods, the Fates, the Furies, and heroes, mortals of the distant past such as <b>Heracles</b>, Theseus and Achilles, who were worshipped at their tombs and could help or harm the living.</blockquote><a href="/religion/ancient-greek-religion">Ancient Greek religion · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li></ul></section>
